<!--
    THE ONBOARDING STAGE

    The root of every one of the eight step pages, and the WPF spelling of
    OnboardingView.swift:79-101:

        GeometryReader { proxy in ScrollView { ZStack { stage }
            .frame(minHeight: proxy.size.height) } }

    The comment there spells out why it is not a plain fixed frame: every step is
    laid out exactly as designed and vertically centred by its own spacers, and
    the scroll view only engages once real content exceeds the sheet. "A Mac with
    several virtual inputs listed on the microphone step, or a long dictation on
    the try it step, would otherwise clip at both ends with no way to reach the
    content."

    A fixed 760 x 624 non-resizable WPF window has exactly the same overflow and
    less forgiveness, so: MinHeight on the content grid tracks the ScrollViewer's
    own ViewportHeight. Short steps stay centred and show no scrollbar; long ones
    scroll. The progress hairline and the footer live in OnboardingWindow and stay
    OUTSIDE this control, so Continue, Back and "Set Up Later" can never be
    scrolled off screen.
-->
<UserControl x:Class="HyperWhisper.Views.Controls.Onboarding.OnboardingStage"
             xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
    <ScrollViewer x:Name="StageScroll"
                  VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                  HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled"
                  Focusable="False">
        <Grid x:Name="StageGrid"
              VerticalAlignment="Stretch"
              MinHeight="{Binding ViewportHeight, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=ScrollViewer}}">
            <ContentPresenter Content="{Binding StageContent, RelativeSource={RelativeSource AncestorType=UserControl}}"
                              VerticalAlignment="Center"
                              HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"/>
        </Grid>
    </ScrollViewer>
</UserControl>
